A||||8||Philphine||subs...10's or 12's?||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||10-11-2001||02:55 AM||||<BR>in my '86 gt. i have decided on a big system (as in big speakers. not really a lot of them). i'm going to try the fierosound method and since sub tech has advanced some i think i can put 12's where he put 10's, or 10's in a smaller space. <P>the 10's i want are rated donw to 25hz freq responce. the 12's down to 20hz. sensitivity close to the same (93 and 94 respectivly). do you think the 5hz difference would be worth going for the 12's? i'm pretty set on the brand, i have some in my truck i like a lot.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||10-11-2001||03:43 AM||||I'm not much of a stereo guru, but I don't necessarily think that bigger is better, especially in a Fiero.  It all depends what you're using the system for, but I have a fairly simple setup and get great sound (although it probably wouldn't be much of a competition system).  I have a good head unit, Kenwood amp, Kenwood 5 1/4" component speakers (in Aus' door pods) and a single 10" Cerwin Vega sub (used to be in one of Aus' center console sub boxes but I'm moving it to the firewall like fierosound has).  Sounds great and it'll blow your ears out if you aren't careful.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rx7speed||10-11-2001||03:55 AM||||go with the 10<BR>though I would recomend the infinity perfects<BR>they are about he best sounding sub I have heard<BR>they go down to 18hz and pull off some clean bass<P>------------------<BR>who needs pistons I have rotary<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hippocrat||10-11-2001||03:57 AM||||&lt;Personal opinion&gt;<P>Id go with the 10s the if you like a bit more punch. In those tight quarters the 12s going 5 lower is only going to make you a little more queasy wont really give you a better sound. <BR>&lt;/personal opinion&gt;<P>And if it comes down to comps? <BR>I run 2 pioneer IMPPs  10" my brother runs the same model 12" I beat him in SPL<P>With the same amps and basically head units.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||10-11-2001||08:15 AM||||i'm pretty much an infinity fan. good balance bettween cost and quality. thats's what i put up front, but perfects have too large a magnet diameter wise and are pretty deep. i may gamble and get some but for now i'm looking at the kappa greens. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||10-11-2001||08:21 AM||||Get the 10"s. Easier to pack and I doubt you can detect a 5Hz difference without instruments.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||10-11-2001||08:29 AM||||Go with the 10" speakers. You won't notice the 5hz because they'll be firing thru the seats anyways. The resonant frequency of the Fiero passenger compartment will give you the lows. The 10" will take up less room also.||||reg||1
Z||000007||86seyellow||10-11-2001||09:26 AM||||I like the 10w. If you look in the last week or so on the forum you can see how I did my sub, really custom and great sound. The post is "my new 10"<BR>Minn Green also has a pic of the install. You dont have much room but it fits like it was made for the car.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||prtzlboy||10-11-2001||01:02 PM||||I'm afraid I must be the one dissenting opinion on this one, I tried a 10 and a 12 in my car and I MUCH preferred the 12.  It gave me all the "punch you in the face" of the 10 with a little extra "tickle you in the tush".  I've never been satisfied with the low frequency response of 10 inch subs, and if you like to listen to any rap at all, you know lower is better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I believe 12" subs are pretty much the best overall performers for people like me who listen to CCR, Lisa Loeb, The Smashing Pumpkins, and Snoop Doggy Dogg :P  Both were older JL w6's running on an Orion HCCA.  The keys to good (low end) sound are:  a good enclosure, and a GREAT amp.  Shoehorning a decent enclosure for a 12 into the fiero is the hard part.||||reg||1
